---
description: "Read docs/design-system.md (and any external DESIGN.md it points at) before any UI work."
---

# Skill: design-aware

> Auto-installed by `genasis attach` under `.claude/genasis/skills/design-aware/`.
> The skill is a thin contract — it points at GENASIS.md and the relevant
> sections of `blueprint.md` rather than restating them.

## When to invoke (always, before any of these)
- Introducing a new color / spacing / typography / radius / shadow token.
- Creating a new UI component.
- Reviewing or PR-checking UI changes.
- Responding to user requests that mention design, style, branding, or theme.

## Reference order (absolute rule)
1. Open `docs/design-system.md`. Inspect the `mode` field of its frontmatter.
2. **`mode: pristine`** → the body of `docs/design-system.md` is the truth.
   Stop here.
3. **`mode: external`** →
   a. Read `docs/design-system/DESIGN.md` first (= §A, the 1st-class truth).
   b. Read §B "User overrides" of `docs/design-system.md` next.
   c. Final decision = (DESIGN.md) ⊕ (§B). §B wins on overlap.
4. The frontmatter also carries `gallery_preview` — surface it to the user
   when they want to see the active design rendered.

## When the user asks for a design / style change
1. Quote the relevant §A item (or pristine body item) **verbatim**, side-by-
   side with the user request.
2. If the request matches §A → proceed. No override is recorded.
3. If §A is silent on the topic → proceed. Optionally suggest a §B entry if
   the request introduces a new convention.
4. If the request **conflicts with §A** → ask explicitly:
   "DESIGN.md says X = `<old>`. You're asking for `<new>` —
    override DESIGN.md? [y/N]"
5. On `y` → run `genasis design override add "<request>"`. The CLI appends
   the entry to §B.2 of `docs/design-system.md` and bumps `override_count`
   in `docs/.design-state.toml`. Then proceed.
6. On `n` → honour §A and offer an alternative that respects it.

## Hard rules
- Never edit `docs/design-system/DESIGN.md` directly. It is read-only.
  To change source, swap to a different external design.
- Never invent hex/px literals. If a token is missing, add it via §B
  override (with user approval) or escalate.
- Never silently override §A. Always surface the conflict and get explicit
  user approval before running `override add`.

## After a design swap (`genasis design swap <slug>` or `--from <path>`)
- The CLI prints next-step guidance. Echo it to the user thread:
  • UI agents will reference docs/design-system.md → §A → §B.
  • Preview link, gallery link, restore command.
- If existing in-progress UI work was based on the previous design, post
  in the relevant Plane issue thread: "📘 design replaced: <old> → <new>".
  Re-check the affected components against the new §A.

## Anti-patterns
- Quoting tokens from memory instead of from the current §A.
- Adding overrides without surfacing the conflict and asking the user.
- Editing `docs/design-system/DESIGN.md` directly (use swap or §B).
- Treating §A and §B as equal — §B is *patch on top*, not a rival truth.
